{- Cross-backend snapshot test for the parser's string-literal escape
   sequences. The parser supports six escapes (see 'Awsum.Parser'): five
   are exercised here verbatim — `\n \t \r \" \\`. The sixth, `\0`
   (NUL), is intentionally omitted: LLVM and WASM treat strings as
   null-terminated bytes (their `__concat` and `__print` runtime helpers
   call `strlen` / `printf("%s", …)`), so a NUL inside a literal
   truncates the printed output, while JVM, CLR, and JS preserve the
   NUL — this is a known cross-backend divergence tracked separately.

   The literal below packs each remaining escape inline. Identical
   stdout bytes on every backend confirm both the parser's escape
   handling and the runtime's "print exactly" semantics; the
   no-stdin snapshot pins the exact byte sequence (including CR / LF
   / TAB characters that look unusual in text editors). -}
import IO.Stdout

main : Either (StringTooLong | UnpairedUtf16Surrogate) String -> IO Never Unit
main _input = IO.Stdout.print "BS=\\ DQ=\" CR=\r LF=\n TAB=\t end"
